# PART 8 — ACCESSIBILITY, PERFORMANCE AND CODE QUALITY

- Use semantic HTML landmarks.
- Use appropriate heading hierarchy.
- Provide accessible names to icon-only buttons.
- Use keyboard-accessible tabs and accordions.
- Use visible focus states.
- Maintain good color contrast.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Optimize SVG and image usage.
- Avoid unnecessarily large images.
- Lazy-load non-critical images when relevant.
- Avoid layout shifts.
- Use reusable components.
- Use strict TypeScript types.
- Keep business data separate from UI components.
- Add comments only where they explain meaningful decisions.
- Do not use emoji anywhere in the application, code, comments, README or documentation.

Do not use unnecessary animation or motion to compensate for weak layout.

